> The models are known. Assume y1=x1+x2+x3; y2=x1^2+x2^2+x3^2; y3=x1^3
> +x2^3+x3^3;
> And assume that observations y1, y2 and y3 are normalized distrubtion,
> specified by u1, v1; u2, v2; and u3, v3, respectively.
>
> Based on the Bayes' thoery:
>
> p(X|D)=(p(D|X)p(X))/p(D)
>
> where p(X|D) is the posterior PDF of parameters x1, x2, x3; p(D|X) is
> the likelihood function, specified by multivariate normal
> distribution, Ny1(u1,v1)* Ny2(u2,v2)* Ny3(u3,v3), where
> Ny1(u1,v1)=1/sqrt(2pi*v1)*exp(-(y1-u1)^2/(2*v1)); p(X) is prior PDF;
> and p(D) is the normalized factor.
>
> Can INLA handle this problem? If so, can you leave a simple example?

INLA cannot handle multivariate Normal observations directly except when
the precision matrix is a diagonal. You can however us this trick.
assume

y = mu + b*x + eps

where eps is bivariate normal. then rewrite this as

y = mu + b*x + eps + eps_1

where eps_1 is considered as the observation noise and eps as a 'random
effect'. then prec(eps_1) is fixed and high, wheras 'eps' can be the
iid2d model, a bivariate normal with conjugate prior for the precision
matrix.

H

